WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.449
The very first thing before we get into
the conversation... I've put together a

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:08.340
little something with some of the
photos that I liked the most from

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.910
the large set that Fred shared with us.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.480
And I want to say a few things over it.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:20.520
So this is 85 photos in 365 seconds 
that I've put together for you.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.040
It's going to let us just dive straight into the work

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.260
and I'm gonna just say a couple things over it to

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.840
hopefully introduce some of the
themes we're gonna go into tonight.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.560
Is that good, Fred?

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.700
Do you want to say anything before I launch into that?

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:39.920
Fred: Yes. Lock the doors don't let anybody out!

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.098
This is your life, Fred Lyon.

00:00:43.098 --> 00:00:45.400
So here we go...

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.460
Fred Lyon and San Francisco.
Our two subjects of the night.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.660
So we'll start with these 85 photos in 365 seconds...

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:02.840
And we talked about how...
photography is really all about time.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.560
So I'm going to say a few words
and stir up the themes.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.060
These postcards from the past...

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.500
Some icons from today's San Francisco
you'll recognize...

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.420
other things that hint at an age gone by.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.140
There's nature and culture and art
and commerce intermingled.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.340
...in a San Francisco we all know well.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.920
It was a night like this long ago.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.420
Let's have a look.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.900
Maybe even gawk.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.460
The frame shows us scenes that are old,
but maybe new to us.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.580
While others are familiar.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.240
Persistent and enduring.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.400
The present decontextualized by seeing
it also existed in the past.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:05.380
Sometimes a dramatic new vantage point.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.220
Or the same again. Enduring.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.740
Change that stays the same.
But different.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.420
Cities are like this.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.540
Stable but dynamic.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.500
Still but in motion.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.480
Like what we call "pace layers" here at Long Now.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.340
Culture and nature up against each other.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.780
An entangling of governance and
infrastructure.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.480
And commerce.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.560
All those parts moving in concert.
And contrast.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.080
Some things fleeting never to return.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.920
Others continuous. The persistent set.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.340
Though costumes may change...
with fashion.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.820
Steps on the temporal path
to where we are now.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.380
Even though we're in the same place all along.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.080
But there are odd artifacts in the midst.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.860
Some are surreal.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.400
Or real, but still strange.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.380
Others seem banal...

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.540
which makes their details irrefutable.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:35.600
The photos remind us there's a story to the moment
and to each face.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.320
And more stories.
Every car connects lives...

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.440
transit... an open window...

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.640
Travel between places... between stories.

00:03:44.642 --> 00:03:47.819
Stories frozen in a moment or a trajectory.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.460
How did the moment end?
What happened next?

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.540
Where are they now?
Around the block or the world?

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.520
Maybe the surreal meeting the ordinary
is actually always present.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.560
The iconic object under maintenance.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.780
Golden Gate Park's glory, framed as a sliver. 
Beside the fact of all our humanity.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.120
A front yard, rare in San Francisco,
with an unnatural glow.

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:16.237
Strange things, maybe from before
you came to town.

00:04:16.237 --> 00:04:19.237
Did you hear about that accordion house?

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.500
Who's this guy?

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.220
There's a story behind each picture-making, too.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.820
Practice. Choices. Luck. And timing.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.580
In a sense we're all in the currents of time.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:38.260
Rushing past in youth.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.380
Changing pace as life goes on.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.900
Our memory is biased to big occasions.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:52.320
So we may miss how sweet
an everyday moment actually is.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.554
Just another day at work.

00:04:57.554 --> 00:05:00.530
The same old commute.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.810
Home sweet home.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:10.440
Each moment every day open to routine 
or epic adventure.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.080
A photo's not worth a thousand words.
It's different.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.360
Sublime. The photo brings feelings in between words.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.200
So I'll stop the words, 
and just give you Fred's photos

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.720
for a little longer...

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.620
85 photos in 365 seconds. We made it!

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:38.360
Fred: Play them again.
Michael: Thanks everybody, have a great night!

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.140
And we end with 4 iconic San Francisco shots.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.360
These are Fred's "skinnies."

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.120
Do you want to say a quick word about skinnies?

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.120
Then we'll go back into...

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.160
Fred: Actually it was an art director

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.720
who was working on a new magazine a long time ago

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:07.360
and he had columns, vertical columns he had to fill.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.780
And he said, do you have any skinny pictures?

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.080
I said, why? He said, well I have a client

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.300
who has some very bad copy. And I thought

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:25.110
if we put a large picture instead of the bad copy...

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:28.980
And so, you know, these are just croppings.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.980
And I thought, hey, this is fun.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.300
And I kind of like the look of them.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.880
And it's a sort of an elegant proportion.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:41.940
It was a great success.
So I've been making them ever since.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.180
It's just a cropping, but

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:49.640
San Francisco is a very vertical town,

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.700
and so why not do vertical pictures?

00:07:54.200 --> 00:08:01.980
It also encouraged me to make a lot of vertical pictures

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:09.800
because I discovered early on,
when I was really starving and...

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.580
I was being paid by page rate.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.620
And I discovered that if I shot horizontals

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.020
the best I could hope for was a half-page.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.500
But if I shot vertical I might get a full page.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.400
And so I really learned to shoot a lot of verticals.

